{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.8,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["D2", "C14", "R3"],
      "pins": ["J5.2", "J5.1"],
      "nets": ["HEATER", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the Microcontroller sheet (mcu.kicad_sch, page 3/4), the heater-indicator branch from the HEATER net (J5 pin 2) to GND (J5 pin 1) consists of D2 (LED) in series with C14, a 22 µF capacitor. There is no series resistor in this branch, and reference R3 does not appear anywhere on the drawing. The position that the reference design fills with a current‑limiting resistor R3 is instead occupied by a capacitor.",
      "expected": "The specification states that the HEATER net carries 'A status LED (D2) and series resistor (R3) ... so the heater output doubles as a visual heater-on indicator'. A LED used as a steady 12 V indicator must be in series with a current‑limiting resistor, not a capacitor.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "A status LED (D2) and series resistor (R3) are also on the HEATER net, so the heater output doubles as a visual 'heater on' indicator at the board level (Section 3, Heater path)."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch (page 3/4), left‑centre: J5 Screw_Terminal_01x02 pin 2 = HEATER -> D2 LED -> C14 22uF -> GND, with no resistor in the branch."}
      ],
      "impact": "The LED will not light continuously when the heater is on. On each heater transition the discharged 22 µF capacitor presents a near‑short across D2, so the LED only flashes momentarily and is exposed to an un‑limited inrush current (possible LED damage); the intended steady 'heater on' visual indication is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board matches the specification on power, thermocouple SPI, heater switching and USB, but the heater‑status indicator is wrong: D2 is placed in series with a 22 µF capacitor (C14) on the HEATER net where the intended design (and sound practice) requires the series current‑limiting resistor R3, so the LED flashes instead of indicating heater state and risks over‑current."
}

